<bx:output>
<h1>Views &amp; Templates</h1>
<p class="bx-docs-lede">res.render(), .bxm templates, layouts and partials.</p>

<h2>Setting up a views directory</h2>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.set( "views", expandPath( "./views" ) )</code></pre>
<p>Every view path passed to <code class="bx-inline">res.render()</code> is resolved against this directory, with a containment check — a view path can't escape it, even if built from user input.</p>

<h2>.bxm templates</h2>
<p>Views are native BoxLang templates: <code class="bx-inline">data</code> is available as a struct, and interpolation needs a <code class="bx-inline">&lt;bx:output&gt;</code> block, same as classic CFML <code class="bx-inline">&lt;cfoutput&gt;</code>. A view name with no extension gets <code class="bx-inline">.bxm</code> appended.</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.get( "/greet/:name", ( req, res ) => {
    res.render( "greeting", { name: req.params.name } )
} )</code></pre>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">html — views/greeting.bxm</span><code>&lt;bx:output&gt;&lt;h1&gt;Hello, ##encodeForHTML( data.name )##!&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/bx:output&gt;</code></pre>
<div class="bx-callout bx-warn">
  <strong>Escape what users send you</strong>
  Output isn't escaped automatically — wrap anything that came from a request in <code class="bx-inline">encodeForHTML()</code>, as above, or a visitor can inject HTML and script into the page.
</div>
<p>Handlebars (<code class="bx-inline">.hbs</code>) views were removed in BoxLang Express 0.2.20; rendering one throws <code class="bx-inline">BoxExpress.UnsupportedViewEngine</code>.</p>

<h2 id="layouts">Layouts</h2>
<p>A layout is an ordinary view that wraps another one: the page shell — <code class="bx-inline">&lt;html&gt;</code>, navigation, footer — written once. Name one for the whole app, and <code class="bx-inline">res.render()</code> renders the view first, then the layout with the view's HTML as <code class="bx-inline">data.body</code>:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.set( "views", expandPath( "./views" ) )
app.set( "view layout", "layouts/site" )      // views/layouts/site.bxm</code></pre>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">html — views/layouts/site.bxm</span><code>&lt;bx:output&gt;&lt;!doctype html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;&lt;title&gt;##data.title## · BoxLang Express&lt;/title&gt;&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;aside class="bx-sidebar"&gt;##partial( "partials/_sidebar" )##&lt;/aside&gt;
  &lt;main&gt;##data.body##&lt;/main&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
&lt;/bx:output&gt;</code></pre>
<p>A page's own view is then only its content — no header or footer includes:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">html — views/docs/some-page.bxm</span><code>&lt;bx:output&gt;
  &lt;h1&gt;Page content&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;/bx:output&gt;</code></pre>
<div class="bx-callout">
  <strong>Try it live</strong>
  Every page of this site is rendered this way. <code class="bx-inline">app.bxs</code> sets <code class="bx-inline">"view layout"</code> once; the page you're reading is <code class="bx-inline">views/docs/views.bxm</code>, wrapped in <code class="bx-inline">views/layouts/site.bxm</code>, whose sidebar is the <code class="bx-inline">partials/_sidebar</code> partial.
</div>
<p>The layout sees the same <code class="bx-inline">data</code> as the view. And because the view runs first, it can pass values up to the layout by setting them on <code class="bx-inline">data</code>:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">html — a view setting its own page title</span><code>&lt;bx:set data.title = "Quarterly report"&gt;
&lt;bx:output&gt;&lt;h1&gt;...&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/bx:output&gt;</code></pre>
<p>One render can choose a different layout, or none, with a <code class="bx-inline">layout</code> key in its data. This site's error pages are complete HTML documents of their own, so they opt out:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>res.render( "login", { layout: "layouts/bare" } )
res.status( 404 ).render( "error", { layout: false, statusCode: 404 } )</code></pre>

<h2 id="partials">Partials</h2>
<p><code class="bx-inline">partial( view, data )</code> renders another view and returns its HTML, for pieces shared between pages. A partial sees everything the calling view does, plus whatever you pass it — and what you pass is gone again when it returns:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">html</span><code>&lt;bx:output&gt;
&lt;bx:loop array="##data.orders##" index="order"&gt;
  ##partial( "partials/order-row", { order: order } )##
&lt;/bx:loop&gt;
&lt;/bx:output&gt;</code></pre>
<p>A plain <code class="bx-inline">&lt;bx:include&gt;</code> still works too, since <code class="bx-inline">.bxm</code> templates are just BoxLang. Its path is relative to the file it's written in rather than to the views directory, and it can't be given data of its own.</p>

<figure class="bx-screenshot">
  <img src="/assets/img/screenshots/docs-preview.png" alt="A docs page rendered inside the site layout, showing the shared sidebar and nav">
  <figcaption>This exact page: its own view wrapped in the shared <code class="bx-inline">layouts/site.bxm</code> layout.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>app.locals / res.locals</h2>
<p>Two plain structs merged into every <code class="bx-inline">render()</code> call's data, so values a route doesn't set explicitly — a site name, the current user, shared nav data — don't have to be threaded through every single <code class="bx-inline">res.render()</code> call by hand. <code class="bx-inline">app.locals</code> is set once, application-wide; <code class="bx-inline">res.locals</code> is set per request (typically from middleware) and only lives for that one request/response cycle. Precedence, low to high: <code class="bx-inline">app.locals</code>, then <code class="bx-inline">res.locals</code> (overriding <code class="bx-inline">app.locals</code>), then whatever you pass as <code class="bx-inline">render()</code>'s own <code class="bx-inline">data</code> argument (overriding both):</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.locals.siteName = "My Site"

app.use( ( req, res, next ) => {
    res.locals.user = req.session.user ?: "guest"
    next()
} )

app.get( "/", ( req, res ) => {
    res.render( "home", {} )   // views/home.bxm sees data.siteName and data.user
} )</code></pre>
<div class="bx-callout">
  <strong>Try it live</strong>
  This documentation site's own nav sidebar is exactly this pattern — <code class="bx-inline">app.locals.navGroups</code> is set once in <code class="bx-inline">app.bxs</code>, and every <code class="bx-inline">/docs/*</code> route renders without passing it explicitly.
</div>

<h2>Quick debugging without a view</h2>
<p>For a one-off inspection route, <code class="bx-inline">res.dump()</code> skips views entirely and sends BoxLang's own rich HTML dump of any variable:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.get( "/debug", ( req, res ) => {
    res.dump( { req: req.query, session: req.session } )
} )</code></pre>

